    transcript:`Sarah: Good morning. You must be Tom. I’m Sarah, the accommodation advisor. How can I help?

Tom: Hi Sarah. I’ve just been accepted for the Master’s in Environmental Science, and I need somewhere to live for the academic year starting in September. I’m not from this city, so I’d prefer on‑campus if possible.

Sarah: Certainly. We have several options. First, there are the traditional dormitories – shared rooms, usually two or three students per room. That’s the cheapest, at about £350 per month, but you share a bathroom and kitchen with the whole floor.

Tom: I’d prefer more privacy. What about single rooms?

Sarah: Yes, we have single ensuite rooms in the new building – Willow Hall. Each room has a private bathroom, and you share a kitchenette with four other students. That costs £550 per month, including all bills except internet, which is an extra £15 per month.

Tom: That sounds reasonable. Is there anything cheaper than that but still private?

Sarah: There are single non‑ensuite rooms in Oak Hall. You have your own room but share a bathroom with six others on the same corridor. That’s £450 per month, bills included. Internet is free there, actually. But Oak Hall is older – no lift, and it’s a ten‑minute walk from the main campus buildings.

Tom: Location matters – my department is in the Science Block. Which hall is closest?

Sarah: Willow Hall is right behind the Science Block – just two minutes. Oak Hall is further, but there’s a free shuttle bus every 20 minutes. Another option is Birch Apartments – these are for postgraduate students only. You get a studio – your own kitchen and bathroom – for £750 per month. Very popular, so there’s often a waiting list.

Tom: Let’s stick with Willow Hall – the ensuite single. How do I apply?

Sarah: You need to complete the online application form on the accommodation portal. You’ll need your student ID number, which is in your offer letter. The deadline for guaranteed accommodation is August 1st. After that, we allocate on a first‑come basis. There’s also a £250 deposit to secure your room, refundable at the end of the year if there’s no damage.

Tom: Is the deposit payable now?

Sarah: Not until you’ve been allocated a room. You’ll receive an offer by email, usually within two weeks of applying. Then you have five working days to accept and pay the deposit. If you miss that, the offer goes to someone else.

Tom: Understood. What about meal plans? Is there a cafeteria?

Sarah: Yes, the central dining hall is in the Student Union building. You can buy a meal plan – 10 meals per week costs £45, 14 meals is £60, and 19 meals (three a day except weekends) is £80. Or you can pay per meal – breakfast £4, lunch £5.50, dinner £7. Many students in Willow Hall cook in their kitchenettes to save money.

Tom: I think I’ll cook. One last thing – can I request a quiet floor? I need to study a lot.

Sarah: Yes, in the application there’s a section for preferences. You can tick ‘quiet living’ – those floors have a rule of no noise after 10 PM. We also have ‘social floors’ if you prefer parties, but I’m guessing not.

Tom: Definitely quiet floor. Thanks Sarah. I’ll apply today.

Sarah: You’re welcome. And if you have any problems, email accommodation@university.ac.uk. Good luck with your course.`,
