    id:"acad-t1-p3", test:"acad-t1", format:"academic", part:3, kind:"dialogue",
    title:`Academic Test 1 · Part 3`,
    context:`Two university students, Emma and James, discussing their group project on renewable energy adoption in rural areas. A tutor briefly joins.`,
    audio:"/ielts/listening/bank/acad-t1-p3.mp3",
    transcript:`Emma: James, have you looked at the survey data we collected last week? I’ve started analysing it, and the numbers are surprising.

James: Not yet. I’ve been focusing on the literature review. What’s surprising?

Emma: So, we surveyed 200 households in three rural villages. We asked if they would be willing to install solar panels if the government paid 50% of the cost. I expected a high yes rate – maybe 80%. But actually, only 42% said yes. The main reason was upfront cost, even with the subsidy. They said 50% still leaves them with £3,000 to £5,000, which is too much.

James: That’s interesting. Did we ask about alternative financing? Like loans or leasing?

Emma: Yes, question 12. For households that said no, we asked: ‘Would you change your mind if a zero‑interest loan were available?’ Then the yes rate jumped to 71%. So financing seems critical.

James: That matches some of the literature I read. There’s a paper by Sardianou and Genoudi from 2018 – they found that in Greece, upfront cost was the biggest barrier, but access to low‑interest loans increased adoption by 300%. We should cite that.

Emma: Good. Also, we found a geographic difference. Villages closer to the main road had higher acceptance – probably because they have better access to information and installers. The remote village had only 28% initial yes, even with the subsidy.

James: So our recommendation could be to target remote areas with both subsidies and mobile installation teams. Maybe also community‑owned solar farms rather than individual panels.

Emma: That’s a great idea. But we need to check the project guidelines. The tutor said we must include a cost‑benefit analysis. Have you done any calculations?

James: Rough ones. For a typical household, solar panels cost about £8,000 installed. They save around £500 per year on electricity bills. So payback period without subsidy is 16 years. With 50% subsidy, payback is 8 years. That’s still long for rural households with low income.

Emma: What about battery storage? The survey didn’t ask that, but maybe we should add a section. Without batteries, they still need the grid at night.

James: Batteries add another £4,000 – then payback becomes 24 years. Not feasible. So maybe the focus should be on grid‑connected systems where they sell excess power during the day.

Emma: That’s what our data suggests. Let me show you the table. Hang on – I’ll share my screen. Can you see it?

James: Yes. Oh, I see – the column for ‘willingness to sell excess to grid’ is high – 83% said yes. So the economic benefit is clearer if they get paid for generation.

Emma: Exactly. Now, the tutor said we need to submit the methodology section by Friday. I’ve written a draft, but I’m unsure about the sampling method. We used convenience sampling – we went door to door. That’s biased, isn’t it?

James: Yes, convenience sampling means we might have missed people who weren’t home, like those who work during the day. That could mean we under‑represented working families. Ideally we would have used stratified random sampling. But for a small project, it’s probably acceptable. We just need to mention it as a limitation.

Emma: Okay, I’ll add that. Also, what about the interview with the village head? Did you transcribe that?

James: Partly. He said that two years ago, a different company tried to install solar panels but did a poor job – panels failed after six months. That created distrust. So community engagement and quality assurance are also important.

(tutor enters)

Tutor: Hi Emma, James. How’s the project going?

Emma: Good, thanks. We’re analysing survey data and finding that financing is the biggest barrier, not lack of interest.

Tutor: That’s a solid finding. Have you considered seasonal variation? Rural households in winter use more electricity for heating. Solar output is lower in winter. Did your survey account for that?

James: We didn’t. That’s a good point. We’ll add it to the discussion section as a factor for further research.

Tutor: Great. Remember, the final report is due April 30th, and your presentation is on May 5th. Each of you speaks for 8 minutes. Make sure your slides are consistent in style. Any questions?

Emma: Should we include raw data in the appendix?

Tutor: Yes, but only summary tables in the main body. The full dataset can go in the appendix. And don’t forget to anonymise it – no names or addresses.

Tutor leaves.

James: Okay, let’s divide tasks. I’ll finish the literature review and the cost‑benefit analysis. You do the methodology and results. We’ll write the introduction and conclusion together. Deal?

Emma: Deal. Let’s meet again on Wednesday at 3 PM.`,
