    id:"acad-t2-p1", test:"acad-t2", format:"academic", part:1, kind:"dialogue",
    title:`Academic Test 2 · Part 1`,
    context:`A university student services office. A student (Maria) is speaking with an advisor (John) about applying for a research grant.`,
    audio:"/ielts/listening/bank/acad-t2-p1.mp3",
    transcript:`John: Good morning, Maria. How can I help you today?

Maria: Hi John. I’m a second‑year PhD student in Chemistry, and I’m hoping to apply for the university’s Early Career Researcher Grant. I’ve never applied for a grant before, so I need some guidance.

John: Of course. The Early Career Researcher Grant is quite competitive, but it’s a great opportunity. The grant provides up to £5,000 for research expenses – things like travel to conferences, purchasing small equipment, or paying for participant fees. You can’t use it for salary or tuition.

Maria: That’s fine. My research involves synthesising new polymers for water filtration. I need to buy some specialised monomers that cost about £1,200. I also want to attend a conference in Germany next June – registration and travel would be about £1,500. And I need to pay for NMR analysis at about £800. That totals £3,500.

John: That’s within the limit. But you need to justify each item. The grant committee wants to know how each expense will advance your research or your career. For the conference, for example, explain what you’ll present and how networking will help.

Maria: I have a draft application. Can you look at it?

John: Sure, but first, let’s check eligibility. You must have completed at least one year of your PhD, and you cannot have received this grant before. Also, you need a supervisor’s letter of support. Have you spoken to your supervisor, Professor Davies?

Maria: Yes, she said she’d write a strong letter. She also said I should apply for the £5,000 maximum even if I don’t need it all, because I might add expenses later.

John: That’s not good advice, actually. The committee prefers realistic budgets. If you ask for £5,000 but only spend £3,500, they might think you didn’t plan carefully. Ask for what you genuinely need.

Maria: Okay, I’ll revise the budget. What’s the deadline?

John: The application deadline is March 15th. That’s six weeks away. You submit online through the research portal. The application requires: a project summary (500 words), a budget breakdown (table format), a timeline, your CV (max 2 pages), and your supervisor’s letter. Also, two academic references – one from your supervisor and one from another faculty member.

Maria: I can get a second reference from my master’s supervisor. She’s still at my previous university. Is that allowed?

John: Yes, as long as they can comment on your research potential. But the referee should ideally be familiar with your current work. Does she know your PhD topic?

Maria: We’ve stayed in touch, and I’ve sent her my recent paper. So yes.

John: Good. Now, the project summary is critical. The committee will read that first. It must clearly state your research question, methodology, expected outcomes, and how the grant will help. Avoid jargon – some committee members are from other disciplines. Also, mention the broader impact – for example, clean water is a global issue.

Maria: My draft summary is quite technical. I’ll rewrite it. Also, what about the timeline? The grant funds activities between April and December of this year. Can I include a conference in June 2026? That’s next year.

John: No, the grant year is fixed. You can only spend the money between April 1st and December 31st of the current year. Any unused funds must be returned. So plan activities within that window.

Maria: Then I’ll need to find a different conference – there’s one in September in the UK. That works.

John: Also, remember that the university requires ethics approval for any research involving human participants or animals. Does your water filtration research involve testing with human samples?

Maria: No, I’m testing with synthetic contaminated water – no human subjects. But I’ll double‑check with the ethics board.

John: Good. Now, the application process: you fill out the online form, upload your documents, and submit. After submission, the committee takes four weeks to review. Successful applicants are notified by email. If you’re successful, you’ll need to sign a grant agreement and attend a short financial management workshop.

Maria: What’s the success rate?

John: Last year, 45 students applied, and 12 received funding – about 27%. So it’s competitive but possible. The strongest applications had clear budgets, strong supervisor letters, and a well‑written summary that linked the research to real‑world problems.

Maria: That’s helpful. Can I send you my draft for feedback before I submit?

John: Yes, I offer 30‑minute consultations every Tuesday and Thursday afternoon. Just book a slot via the student portal. Bring a printed copy of your draft. And please send me your budget spreadsheet in advance so I can review it.

Maria: Thank you, John. I’ll book for next Tuesday.

John: You’re welcome. Good luck with the application.`,
