    id:"gen-t6-p3", test:"gen-t6", format:"general", part:3, kind:"dialogue",
    title:`General Test 6 · Part 3`,
    context:`Two colleagues, Rachel and Simon, are discussing a problem with their office’s heating and air conditioning system. Their facilities manager, Mr. Dawson, joins later.`,
    audio:"/ielts/listening/bank/gen-t6-p3.mp3",
    transcript:`Rachel: Simon, have you noticed how cold it is in our office? My fingers are numb. The thermostat says 16°C, but it’s set to 21°C. I think the heating is broken.

Simon: Yes, it’s freezing. I checked the radiators – they’re warm at the bottom but cold at the top. That usually means air in the system. We need to bleed them. But I don’t have a radiator key.

Rachel: I have one at home – I can bring it tomorrow. But also, the air conditioning is making a strange noise – a rattling sound from the ceiling vent. And the airflow is weak. Maybe the filter is clogged.

Simon: That’s a different issue. The heating is water‑based; the AC is separate. We should report both to facilities. I’ll email Mr. Dawson now.

(Typing sounds)

Simon: I sent it. He usually replies within an hour.

(Later, Mr. Dawson arrives)

Mr. Dawson: Hi Rachel, Simon. I got your email. Let me check the heating first. (Puts hand on radiator) You’re right – cold at the top. That’s air. I have a key. (He bleeds the radiator – hissing sound) There we go. Once water comes out, I close it. The pressure will drop – I need to repressurise the boiler. Give it 30 minutes, and you should feel heat.

Rachel: Thank you. What about the air conditioning rattling?

Mr. Dawson: That’s a different system. (Listens) That rattling could be a loose fan blade or a foreign object in the duct. I’m not authorised to open the ceiling – that’s a specialist contractor. I’ll call our HVAC company. They usually come within 48 hours. In the meantime, turn off the AC for that zone using the control panel near the door.

Simon: We did – but the noise stopped when we turned it off. Is it safe to use?

Mr. Dawson: If it’s just a rattle, probably safe. But if you hear grinding or screeching, turn it off immediately. I’ll ask the contractor to prioritise you. Also, when was the last filter change? If the filter is clogged, that reduces airflow and makes the fan work harder, which can cause noise.

Rachel: I don’t know. We’ve been here 8 months and never seen it changed.

Mr. Dawson: Filters should be changed every 3 months. I’ll check the maintenance log. If it’s overdue, I’ll change it myself – it’s easy. (Climbs on a chair, opens the vent) Yes, the filter is grey – should be white. I have a spare. (Changes filter) That might help with airflow. The rattle might still be there, but at least the air will be cleaner.

Simon: Thanks. Now, another problem – the thermostat in our zone seems inaccurate. We set it to 21°C, but the actual temperature is 16°C. Is the thermostat faulty?

Mr. Dawson: Possibly, but first, is the thermostat located near a draught or a heat source? It’s on the wall near the window – cold air from the window might make it read low, so it keeps calling for heat, but the radiators can’t keep up because the window is leaky.

Rachel: That window does have a draught – I can feel cold air coming through the frame. Can we seal it?

Mr. Dawson: Yes, I have weatherstripping tape – foam with adhesive. I’ll apply it to the window frame. Also, the thermostat might need recalibration. There’s a small screw inside – I can adjust it. But let’s seal the window first and see if the temperature stabilises.

Simon: Good. Also, some desks are colder than others – the desks near the window are freezing, while desks near the interior wall are fine. Can we balance the radiators?

Mr. Dawson: Yes, there’s a lockshield valve on each radiator – you can adjust it to send more hot water to the colder radiators. I’ll show you. (Shows them) Turn the cap clockwise to close, anti‑clockwise to open. Normally, radiators furthest from the boiler should be fully open, those closest partially closed. I’ll do that now.

Rachel: That’s very helpful. While you’re here, the hot water tap in the kitchen takes 2 minutes to run hot – that’s wasteful. Is that normal?

Mr. Dawson: In a large office building, yes – the water has to travel from the boiler. But I can install a point‑of‑use instant water heater under the sink – about £100 plus installation. I’ll request budget approval. In the meantime, use the kettle for small amounts of hot water.

Simon: Understood. One more issue – the extractor fan in the toilet is very loud and doesn’t seem to extract – the mirror is still steamy after a shower.

Mr. Dawson: That fan might be clogged with dust. I’ll clean it today. If that doesn’t work, it may need replacement. I’ll check the motor bearings. Thanks for reporting these – you’re helping maintain the building.

Rachel: No problem. We appreciate your quick response.

Mr. Dawson: I’ll check back in an hour to see if the heating has improved. If not, I’ll call a plumber. Goodbye for now.`,
