    id:"gen-t6-p4", test:"gen-t6", format:"general", part:4, kind:"monologue",
    title:`General Test 6 · Part 4`,
    context:`A talk at a public library about understanding and reducing stress in daily life. The speaker, Dr. Emily White, is a clinical psychologist.`,
    audio:"/ielts/listening/bank/gen-t6-p4.mp3",
    transcript:`Dr. White: Good evening. I’m Dr. Emily White. Tonight I want to talk about stress – not the clinical kind, but the everyday pressure that most of us feel from work, family, finances, and health. A certain amount of stress is normal – it motivates us. But chronic stress damages our bodies and minds. The good news is that we can manage it with simple techniques. Let’s start with the biology.

When you perceive a threat, your brain triggers the ‘fight or flight’ response. Your heart rate increases, muscles tense, and cortisol (a stress hormone) rises. This response evolved for physical dangers – like a predator. But today, the threats are psychological – a deadline, a difficult email, a traffic jam. Your body still reacts as if you’re being chased by a lion. Over time, high cortisol leads to high blood pressure, poor sleep, weight gain, anxiety, and depression.

So what can you do? The first and most effective is physical activity. Exercise releases endorphins – natural mood elevators. Even a 10‑minute brisk walk reduces cortisol for up to 2 hours. Ideally, aim for 30 minutes of moderate activity (like brisk walking, cycling, or swimming) five times a week. If you can’t, break it into 10‑minute chunks. Exercise also improves sleep, which in turn reduces stress.

Second, breathing. When stressed, you tend to breathe shallowly and rapidly. That signals your brain that something is wrong, perpetuating the cycle. Try ‘box breathing’: inhale for 4 counts, hold for 4, exhale for 4, hold for 4. Repeat for 2 minutes. This activates the parasympathetic nervous system – the ‘rest and digest’ mode. Do this whenever you feel overwhelmed – before a meeting, after a difficult call, or in bed before sleep.

Third, mindfulness. This doesn’t mean emptying your mind. It means paying attention to the present moment without judgement. For example, while drinking tea, focus on the warmth of the cup, the aroma, the taste. When your mind wanders (it will), gently bring it back. There are free apps like Smiling Mind or Insight Timer with 5‑minute guided exercises. Research shows that 10 minutes of mindfulness a day for 8 weeks changes brain structure – reducing the size of the amygdala (fear centre) and increasing the prefrontal cortex (decision making).

Fourth, social connection. Isolating makes stress worse. Even a 5‑minute chat with a friend – in person or by phone – reduces cortisol. But avoid ‘co‑rumination’ – repeatedly complaining without problem‑solving. That actually increases stress. Instead, talk about solutions or just share a laugh. Laughter triggers endorphins and reduces stress hormones. Watch a funny video or call a friend who makes you laugh.

Fifth, sleep hygiene. Most adults need 7‑9 hours. Stress makes it hard to sleep, and poor sleep increases stress – a vicious cycle. Set a consistent bedtime and wake time, even on weekends. Avoid screens (phone, tablet, TV) for an hour before bed – blue light suppresses melatonin. Instead, read a paper book, listen to calm music, or take a warm bath. Keep your bedroom cool (18°C), dark, and quiet. If you can’t sleep after 20 minutes, get up and do something boring (like folding laundry) until you feel tired. Don’t just lie there worrying.

Sixth, time management. Many stressors come from feeling overwhelmed. Use the ‘Eisenhower Matrix’: sort tasks into four boxes – urgent and important (do now), important but not urgent (schedule), urgent but not important (delegate), and neither (eliminate). Most people spend too much time on urgent but not important tasks – like answering every email immediately. Learn to say no. Also, break large tasks into small steps. ‘Write report’ is daunting; ‘open document and write heading’ is easy.

Seventh, challenge unhelpful thoughts. Stress often comes from irrational beliefs. For example, ‘I must be perfect’ or ‘everything is going wrong’. Ask yourself: ‘Is that really true? What’s the evidence? What would I tell a friend in this situation?’ This is cognitive behavioural therapy (CBT). You can learn basic CBT from free online resources or books like ‘Feeling Good’ by David Burns.

Eighth, diet. Caffeine and sugar can mimic stress – they increase heart rate and cause energy crashes. Reduce coffee to one cup in the morning. Avoid energy drinks and sugary snacks. Instead, eat regular meals with protein and complex carbohydrates – like oatmeal, eggs, or nuts. Stay hydrated – dehydration causes fatigue and irritability.

Ninth, nature. Spending time in green space reduces cortisol. Even a view of trees from a window helps. If you can, walk in a park on your lunch break. If not, bring a plant into your office. In one study, hospital patients with a view of trees recovered faster than those with a view of a brick wall.

Tenth, professional help. If stress is interfering with your work, relationships, or sleep for more than a few weeks, see your GP. They can refer you for counselling – often free on the NHS (though waiting lists can be long). In some cases, medication like SSRIs can help, especially if stress has become depression or anxiety disorder. There’s no shame – it’s a medical condition.

To summarise: move your body, breathe deeply, practice mindfulness, connect with others, sleep well, manage time, challenge thoughts, eat healthily, spend time in nature, and seek help if needed. Start with one technique – just five minutes of breathing today. Small changes compound.

Thank you. I’ll now take questions.

End of Test 6 (Academic + General)`,
