    id:"gen-t7-p1", test:"gen-t7", format:"general", part:1, kind:"dialogue",
    title:`General Test 7 · Part 1`,
    context:`A council housing office. A tenant (Mr. Patel) is speaking with an officer (Claire) about a repair request and rent arrears.`,
    audio:"/ielts/listening/bank/gen-t7-p1.mp3",
    transcript:`Claire: Good morning, Mr. Patel. You wanted to discuss a repair to your kitchen ceiling?

Mr. Patel: Yes, there’s a water stain that’s getting bigger. The ceiling is bulging – I think a pipe is leaking in the flat above. This has been going on for three weeks. I reported it twice, but no one has come.

Claire: I’m sorry. Let me check the log. (Typing) I see the first report on March 10th. The contractor was supposed to inspect on March 15th, but they marked ‘no access’ – did you not answer the door?

Mr. Patel: I was at work! I left a key with my neighbour, and I told the call centre to pass that message. They didn’t.

Claire: I’ll update your file: permission to access via neighbour – Mrs. Singh at flat 4. Give me her phone number in case. Also, I’ll escalate this to urgent. A leaking pipe can cause electrical hazards if water reaches wiring. I’ll call the contractor now.

(Pause – phone call)

Claire: They can come tomorrow between 10 AM and 2 PM. Can Mrs. Singh be available?

Mr. Patel: Yes, she’s retired and always home. I’ll tell her. What will they do?

Claire: They’ll inspect, find the leak source, and repair it. If it’s a pipe from upstairs, they’ll need access to that flat too. Do you know the tenant above? Flat 5?

Mr. Patel: Yes, Mr. Jones. He works nights – he’s home in the mornings. I’ll warn him.

Claire: Good. If the ceiling needs replastering, that’s a separate repair. After the leak is fixed, you’ll need to book a plasterer. But first, stop the leak.

Mr. Patel: Now, the second issue – I received a letter saying I owe £450 in rent. But I pay by standing order. My bank shows payments every month. Let me show you. (Shows phone) See – £450 on the 1st of each month.

Claire: Let me check our system. (Typing) I see – you pay £450 per month, but your rent is £550 per month. It went up in April last year. Did you not get the rent increase notice?

Mr. Patel: I got a letter, but I thought it was for the flat above – different address. I didn’t open it carefully. So I’ve been underpaying by £100 per month for 12 months – that’s £1,200, not £450.

Claire: The letter says £450 arrears – that’s the amount for the last 4.5 months, because we only pursue after 3 months. But you actually owe £1,200 total. We can set up a repayment plan.

Mr. Patel: I can’t afford to pay all at once. What’s the plan?

Claire: You can add £50 per month to your rent – so pay £600 per month for 24 months – that clears the £1,200. Or you can pay a lump sum of £300 now, then £50 extra for 18 months. Or you can apply for a Discretionary Housing Payment – that’s a grant from the council to cover arrears if you’re on a low income.

Mr. Patel: What’s your income threshold?

Claire: You need to be on Universal Credit or Pension Credit, or have a sudden drop in income. Are you?

Mr. Patel: No, I work full‑time. So I’ll take the £50 extra per month for 24 months. Can I set up a new standing order?

Claire: Yes, change your standing order to £600 per month starting next month. Also, the arrears of £1,200 will show as a debt, but as long as you keep paying the extra, we won’t take legal action. I’ll send you a repayment agreement by email – sign and return.

Mr. Patel: Will this affect my credit score?

Claire: No, council rent arrears are not reported to credit agencies unless we go to court. We won’t. So your credit is safe.

Mr. Patel: Thank you. Now, one more thing – my garden fence fell down in the storm. The council owns the fence, right?

Claire: Let me check your tenancy agreement. For flats, fences are council responsibility. For houses, sometimes tenants are responsible. You’re in a house? What’s your address?

Mr. Patel: 12 Meadow Road – it’s a terraced house.

Claire: That’s council‑owned. The fence is our responsibility. I’ll raise a repair order. However, fencing is low priority – it might take 4‑6 weeks. In the meantime, secure the area with rope or warning tape if there’s a hazard.

Mr. Patel: It’s not a safety hazard – it’s the back fence to the alley. I’ll wait. Thank you, Claire.

Claire: You’re welcome. Remember to pay the new standing order from May 1st. Any issues, call this direct line.`,
